{
  "meta": {
    "level": "A2",
    "section": "Verbs",
    "number": 6,
    "total_in_level": 10,
    "title": "Future and Ongoing Actions",
    "subtitle": "Ir a + infinitive, estar + gerund, acabar de, volver a and other verb phrases",
    "footer": "Spanish Learning Program · A2 · Verbs · Future and ongoing actions",
    "next": "Next: A2 · PDF 7 — The imperative (basic)"
  },
  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "callout",
      "kind": "tip",
      "title": "After this PDF you can",
      "bullets": [
        "talk about plans and the near future with ir a + infinitive",
        "say what is happening right now with estar + gerund",
        "say that something has just happened (acabar de) or happens again (volver a)",
        "use llevar, seguir, empezar a and dejar de to describe how long and how things develop"
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "h1",
      "text": "1. Plans and the near future: ir a + infinitive"
    },
    {
      "type": "p",
      "text": "Conjugate <b>ir</b> in the present tense, add <b>a</b>, then the <b>infinitive</b>: <i>voy a estudiar</i> (I am going to study). Use it for plans, intentions and predictions you can see coming."
    },
    {
      "type": "conj",
      "tense": "present",
      "verb": "ir",
      "meaning": "to go (+ a + infinitive)",
      "label": "ir a + infinitive",
      "forms": [
        "*voy*",
        "*vas*",
        "*va*",
        "*vamos*",
        "*vais*",
        "*van*"
      ],
      "examples": [
        [
          "Voy a estudiar esta noche.",
          "I am going to study tonight."
        ],
        [
          "¿Vas a venir a la fiesta?",
          "Are you going to come to the party?"
        ],
        [
          "Ella va a viajar a Perú.",
          "She is going to travel to Peru."
        ],
        [
          "Vamos a cenar fuera.",
          "We are going to eat out."
        ],
        [
          "¿Vais a comprar un coche?",
          "Are you (all) going to buy a car?"
        ],
        [
          "Mis padres van a vender la casa.",
          "My parents are going to sell the house."
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "bullets",
      "items": [
        "<b>Negative:</b> <i>No voy a ir.</i> (I am not going to go.)",
        "<b>Predictions:</b> <i>Va a llover.</i> (It is going to rain.) <i>Va a ser difícil.</i>",
        "<b>Suggestion:</b> <i>¡Vamos a comer!</i> means \"let's eat\" as well as \"we are going to eat\".",
        "<b>The present tense also works</b> for fixed plans: <i>Mañana trabajo.</i> <i>El sábado voy a Madrid.</i>"
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "table",
      "style": "plain",
      "widths": [
        5.4,
        5.8,
        5.8
      ],
      "head": [
        "Future time words",
        "Example",
        "English"
      ],
      "rows": [
        [
          "mañana, pasado mañana",
          "Mañana voy a descansar.",
          "Tomorrow I am going to rest."
        ],
        [
          "esta noche, luego, más tarde",
          "Luego vamos a llamarte.",
          "Later we are going to call you."
        ],
        [
          "la semana que viene",
          "La semana que viene van a venir.",
          "Next week they are going to come."
        ],
        [
          "el próximo año / mes / verano",
          "El próximo año voy a estudiar francés.",
          "Next year I am going to study French."
        ],
        [
          "dentro de dos días",
          "Voy a llegar dentro de dos días.",
          "I will arrive in two days."
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "callout",
      "kind": "dutch",
      "title": "Almost the same as Dutch",
      "bullets": [
        "<i>Ik ga studeren</i> = <b>voy a estudiar</b>. Do not forget the <b>a</b>.",
        "<i>over twee dagen</i> (future) = <b>dentro de dos días</b>. <i>en dos días</i> means \"within the course of two days\" and is a different idea."
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "h1",
      "text": "2. Actions in progress: estar + gerund"
    },
    {
      "type": "p",
      "text": "Use <b>estar</b> in the present + the <b>gerund</b> (<i>gerundio</i>) for something happening <b>right now</b> or in this period: <i>Estoy cocinando.</i> (I am cooking.)"
    },
    {
      "type": "p",
      "text": "Regular gerunds: <b>-ar</b> verbs take <b>-ando</b>, <b>-er</b> and <b>-ir</b> verbs take <b>-iendo</b>. The gerund never changes for gender or number."
    },
