    {
      "type": "callout",
      "kind": "dutch",
      "title": "Dutch aan het",
      "text": "<i>Ik ben aan het koken</i> = <b>Estoy cocinando</b>. For habits, Spanish uses the plain present (<i>Cocino todos los días</i>), just like Dutch. The gerund is for the action in progress."
    },
    {
      "type": "callout",
      "kind": "warn",
      "title": "Pronouns with the gerund",
      "text": "Reflexive and object pronouns go <b>in front of estar</b> or <b>attached to the gerund</b> (and then the gerund gets an accent): <b>Me estoy duchando</b> = <b>Estoy duchándome</b>. Never between: <i>Estoy me duchando</i>."
    },
    {
      "type": "h1",
      "text": "3. Just done, doing again, how long"
    },
    {
      "type": "p",
      "text": "<b>acabar de + infinitive</b> = to have just done something:"
    },
    {
      "type": "table",
      "style": "plain",
